Symbolic Link (Symlink)
 = A shortcut.
 It points to the original file. If the original is deleted, the symlink breaks.

🧬 Hard Link
 = A clone.
 It’s another name for the same content. Even if the original is deleted, the hard link still works!

I used ln -s for symlinks and ln for hard links to test it out myself.


Key Differences Between Hard and Symbolic Links
🪢 Hard Link
*Points directly to the inode (the file’s data on disk).
*Both the original and the hard link share the same inode number.
*If the original file is deleted, the hard link still works — because the data still exists.
*Cannot link directories.
*Cannot link files across different filesystems or partitions.

🔗 Symbolic Link (Soft Link)
*A separate file that points to the original file by name/path.
*If the original is deleted, the symlink becomes broken (dangling).
*Can link directories.
*Can link across filesystems.

It was fun breaking things (on purpose!) and seeing what survives 😄
